WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4398.

2
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.000
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
<v JENNIFER CRAMER>I’m not used to this thing.

4
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Oh, wait . . . No.

5
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.500
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>Yeah there we go.

6
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.000
Put it on that side.

7
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.500
<v CRAMER>Okay.

8
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Some . . . does that work?

9
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.400
<v WILLCOX>Yes.

10
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.200
<v CRAMER>Let me get all my facts straight.

11
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.600
I’ve got . . . I just had . . . Oh, did I give you his . . .?

12
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.000
<v WILLCOX>No.

13
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.600
<v CRAMER>I just had it.

14
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.200
Oh, I got it.

15
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.200
I got two of them.

16
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.000
It’s the end of the day.

17
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.911
<v DONALD RAY BRAXTON>So what I’m saying you guys going to edit the tape?

18
00:00:34.911 --> 00:00:35.267
And . . .

19
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:37.756
<v CRAMER>No, we’re not going to edit it.

20
00:00:37.756 --> 00:00:42.378
<v BRAXTON>Well what I’m saying is you’re going to match it in with everything.

21
00:00:42.378 --> 00:00:44.156
<v CRAMER>What we’re going to do . . .

22
00:00:44.156 --> 00:00:47.000
<v BRAXTON>You’re going to make it blend in.

23
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:47.586
<v CRAMER>We are.

24
00:00:47.586 --> 00:00:54.034
We’re going to . . . What we’re going to do is . . . So this is a part one of a potential part two step, right?

25
00:00:54.034 --> 00:00:54.328
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

26
00:00:54.328 --> 00:00:59.017
<v CRAMER>So we’re going to do this really brief interview, just asking you a couple of things.

27
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:02.828
Then if you like . . . If you enjoy the experience and you don’t mind . . .

28
00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:04.000
<v BRAXTON>Y’all come back.

29
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.889
<v CRAMER>We’ll probably come back to your home, where it’s quiet and you’re comfortable and we’ll talk for like an hour,

30
00:01:08.889 --> 00:01:11.089
an hour and a half, something along those lines.

31
00:01:11.089 --> 00:01:15.978
This is just us kind of doing some fact finding and giving you an opportunity to get to know us,

32
00:01:15.978 --> 00:01:18.178
and us an opportunity to get to know you.

33
00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:18.422
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

34
00:01:18.422 --> 00:01:23.311
<v CRAMER>And then we’ll come up . . . another thing is we’ll send you a . . . you walk away with a copy of this.

35
00:01:23.311 --> 00:01:25.022
We’re going to burn it right here.

36
00:01:25.022 --> 00:01:25.511
And it . . .

37
00:01:25.511 --> 00:01:26.000
<v WILLCOX>CDs.

38
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.831
<v CRAMER>The CDs are right here, and then we’ve got another recorder that’s . . . It’s actually a little bit better audio.

39
00:01:30.831 --> 00:01:33.373
It’s the one that kind of leads directly to this.

40
00:01:33.373 --> 00:01:38.712
So if you hear a lot of background noise on this one, this is just the one we’re doing for today.

41
00:01:38.712 --> 00:01:40.492
We’ve got one with like lots less . . .

42
00:01:40.492 --> 00:01:41.000
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

43
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.319
<v CRAMER>Like the . . . That heater you can hear, the people giggling in the back . . .

44
00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:44.574
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

45
00:01:44.574 --> 00:01:45.340
<v CRAMER>So don’t worry.

46
00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:46.106
There’s another version.

47
00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:50.702
The other things we’ll do is we’ll send you a compilation of everybody’s stories that were told today.

48
00:01:50.702 --> 00:01:50.957
<v BRAXTON>Really?

49
00:01:50.957 --> 00:01:51.213
<v CRAMER>Yes!

50
00:01:51.213 --> 00:01:51.979
If you’re interested.

51
00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:53.000
<v BRAXTON>On one CD?

52
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:53.271
<v CRAMER>Yes!

53
00:01:53.271 --> 00:02:01.686
So that’s why we ask for your address and everything so we can keep in touch with you and let you know how the project’s going and that kind of thing.

54
00:02:01.686 --> 00:02:01.957
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

55
00:02:01.957 --> 00:02:07.114
<v CRAMER>And then the other thing that we’ll do if you’re interested is send you any photographs that Charlotte takes.

56
00:02:07.114 --> 00:02:10.914
So we’ll send you that on a CD too if you’re interested in that.

57
00:02:10.914 --> 00:02:12.000
<v BRAXTON>Photographs like what?

58
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.516
<v CRAMER>She’s going to take . . . If you don’t mind, she’s going to take a picture of us doing the interview.

59
00:02:17.516 --> 00:02:18.097
<v BRAXTON>Oh, okay.

60
00:02:18.097 --> 00:02:18.968
<v CRAMER>Is that okay?

61
00:02:18.968 --> 00:02:20.419
<v BRAXTON>I guess I look okay.

62
00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:21.000
<v WILLCOX>Yes!

63
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:21.739
<v CRAMER>You look fabulous.

64
00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:22.971
I mean oh, my goodness.

65
00:02:22.971 --> 00:02:24.696
I’ve been up since five this morning.

66
00:02:24.696 --> 00:02:26.667
Goodness knows . . . Don’t take a picture of me.

67
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:27.899
So, let’s see what else.

68
00:02:27.899 --> 00:02:32.826
Oh, and then we’re going to give you a copy of this and you can . . . To sort of review it.

69
00:02:32.826 --> 00:02:37.507
So if we got something incorrect or something like that . . . You have an opportunity to kind of correct it.

70
00:02:37.507 --> 00:02:38.000
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

71
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.892
<v CRAMER>And let us know for different versions.

72
00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:42.865
But then like I said, you’re going to have take two.

73
00:02:42.865 --> 00:02:45.838
We’re going to come back and do a much longer one.

74
00:02:45.838 --> 00:02:46.108
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

75
00:02:46.108 --> 00:02:48.000
<v CRAMER>Does that answer most of your questions?

76
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.270
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

77
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:48.541
<v CRAMER>Okay.

78
00:02:48.541 --> 00:02:57.459
Then we’ll have at the end . . . we’re going to have a release form that says I know Jennifer was taping me and it’s okay and I’m giving copyright to LSU [Louisiana State University].

79
00:02:57.459 --> 00:02:58.000
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

80
00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.506
<v CRAMER>Does that sound okay?

81
00:02:59.506 --> 00:02:59.883
Alright.

82
00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:05.532
<v WILLCOX>Do you mind if I snap a few pictures to post to our Facebook page?

83
00:03:05.532 --> 00:03:07.792
A little summary of the interview?

84
00:03:07.792 --> 00:03:10.429
<v BRAXTON>What’s all going to be on Facebook?

85
00:03:10.429 --> 00:03:16.831
<v CRAMER>Your . . . It would be like the . . . It would be like a little paragraph saying . . . It will say,

86
00:03:16.831 --> 00:03:27.000
“Donald Braxton sat down today with Jennifer Cramer on Mossville Oral History Day and they talked about him growing up in Mossville and they enjoyed themselves.”

87
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:27.233
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

88
00:03:27.233 --> 00:03:28.867
<v CRAMER>That’s really what it’s going to say.

89
00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:29.567
Is that okay?

90
00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:32.133
<v WILLCOX>It’s going to be a really brief, not even a thing.

91
00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:33.533
<v CRAMER>It’s just to let people know . . .

92
00:03:33.533 --> 00:03:34.000
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

93
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.860
<v CRAMER>So that when your friends see it and they get jealous and they’re like, “Aw I want to go do that.”

94
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:40.140
Right?

95
00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:40.977
Or your families . . .

96
00:03:40.977 --> 00:03:41.814
<v BRAXTON>I don’t know.

97
00:03:41.814 --> 00:03:43.488
<v CRAMER>Like wants another picture of you.

98
00:03:43.488 --> 00:03:44.326
<v BRAXTON>I don’t know.

99
00:03:44.326 --> 00:03:46.000
I got some bad friends.

100
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:46.412
<v CRAMER>Okay.

101
00:03:46.412 --> 00:03:50.941
So . . . Alright I guess I’ll . . . We’ll just go ahead and start.

102
00:03:50.941 --> 00:03:52.176
Are we on?

103
00:03:52.176 --> 00:03:52.588
<v WILLCOX>[Agrees].

104
00:03:52.588 --> 00:03:55.882
<v CRAMER>So today is January twenty-third the year 2015.

105
00:03:55.882 --> 00:04:05.353
And I am Jennifer Cramer and I’m here representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

106
00:04:05.353 --> 00:04:13.176
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

107
00:04:13.176 --> 00:04:14.000
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

108
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:21.333
<v CRAMER>And to kick that off, we’re holding Mossville Oral History Day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center in Mossville, Louisiana.

109
00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:26.100
And today I have the fortune of sitting down with Donald R. Braxton.

110
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.233
And I’m so grateful that you are sitting down with us for this interview.

111
00:04:31.233 --> 00:04:33.800
And it’s going to be real short.

112
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:34.167
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

113
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:40.033
<v CRAMER>And we’ll likely be in touch with you to set up a longer one for later.

114
00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:42.600
So thank you again for being here.

115
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.533
And can you please state your full name?

116
00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:47.000
<v BRAXTON>Donald Ray Braxton.

117
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.020
<v CRAMER>And when and where were you born?

118
00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:55.040
<v BRAXTON>I was born March the sixth, 1951.

119
00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:01.356
I’m pretty sure I went to the hospital in Lake Charles,

120
00:05:01.356 --> 00:05:12.267
but I stayed the remainder of my life over here in the . . . In Mossville with my mom and dad.

121
00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:16.287
And we stayed on . . . what’s it called?

122
00:05:16.287 --> 00:05:23.752
Old Spanish Trail, but they done changed that named to Burton I think.

123
00:05:23.752 --> 00:05:24.901
Burton Street.

124
00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:29.495
Well we stayed there for years and years.

125
00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:32.366
My dad worked two jobs.

126
00:05:32.366 --> 00:05:45.000
He was a male nurse for Sulphur, which was just Sulphur Hospital, and now they done changed it to something else.

127
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:53.429
<v CRAMER>So let me interrupt you to ask you what your parents’ names were.

128
00:05:53.429 --> 00:05:57.319
<v BRAXTON>My dad name was Ollie Braxton.

129
00:05:57.319 --> 00:06:03.154
He was the son of Nolan and Marcella Rigmaiden.

130
00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:12.879
Okay, which he had . . . there was three, four, you see . . . one sister and two brothers.

131
00:06:12.879 --> 00:06:17.418
The one sister was named [Luticia?] Rigmaiden.

132
00:06:17.418 --> 00:06:27.143
The other brother, which was younger than my dad, his name was [El?] Cedric Rigmaiden.

133
00:06:27.143 --> 00:06:31.681
The brother that was older than my

134
00:06:31.681 --> 00:06:40.110
<c.p>dad was named Nolan Braxton, which was named after my grandfather Nolan Braxton.

135
00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:51.132
Okay, but my great grandfather which is kind of a problem, but his name is Augusta Braxton.

136
00:06:51.132 --> 00:07:04.747
And Augusta, I didn’t know of him because evidently I . . . he had passed by the time I was born or whatever,

137
00:07:04.747 --> 00:07:11.231
but I knew all my other aunts and my uncle.

138
00:07:11.231 --> 00:07:15.121
I guess I’m doing pretty good.

139
00:07:15.121 --> 00:07:17.066
I don’t know.

140
00:07:17.066 --> 00:07:19.011
<v CRAMER>You’re doing fantastic.

141
00:07:19.011 --> 00:07:21.604
I really appreciate it.

142
00:07:21.604 --> 00:07:23.549
Because we do.

143
00:07:23.549 --> 00:07:36.516
We want to go back and get as many . . . As much information about your parents and your grandparents as possible.

144
00:07:36.516 --> 00:07:43.000
What is your earliest memory of Mossville growing up?

145
00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:52.073
<v BRAXTON>My earliest was just standing down the road down the Old Spanish Trail and little house in front of my aunt,

146
00:07:52.073 --> 00:07:57.258
who was what we call Aunt Martill, we had a big ditch.

147
00:07:57.258 --> 00:08:05.035
And I’m pretty sure I was about . . . I’m really thinking because it seemed like I was about four,

148
00:08:05.035 --> 00:08:10.652
maybe five years old as far as I can go back to remember.

149
00:08:10.652 --> 00:08:17.997
And in this ditch we had crawfish and I used to go out there all the time.

150
00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:25.774
And when the ditch was full, the crawfish come out and I would get a piece of meat,

151
00:08:25.774 --> 00:08:33.983
a piece of bacon, or whatever, and put it on a line and go out there and catch crawfish.

152
00:08:33.983 --> 00:08:40.895
And I did that for so long and years passed and then I had . . . My mom,

153
00:08:40.895 --> 00:08:46.080
my dad had a kid, another child, which was Sandra, my sister.

154
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.240
She’s younger than I am.

155
00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:55.153
And I can remember catching crawfish and my mom or my dad would leave the house,

156
00:08:55.153 --> 00:09:00.338
and either my mom would be asleep, and me there with her.

157
00:09:00.338 --> 00:09:08.115
And I would pull the crawfish out the bucket and scare my sister and run around the house.

158
00:09:08.115 --> 00:09:08.547
But . . .

159
00:09:08.547 --> 00:09:08.979
<v CRAMER>[Laughs].

160
00:09:08.979 --> 00:09:10.707
With the little claws . . .

161
00:09:10.707 --> 00:09:26.693
<v BRAXTON>They used to get on me because they’ll find a couple of crawfish dead because it was behind the ice box or . . . I couldn’t find them to pick them up and put them back in the bucket.

162
00:09:26.693 --> 00:09:30.150
But that’s some of the stuff I remember.

163
00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:34.470
Going to church right there at Mount Zion [Baptist Church].

164
00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:38.359
It’s just so much that used to goes on.

165
00:09:38.359 --> 00:09:43.111
<v CRAMER>So you said you grew up on Old Spanish Trail, where?

166
00:09:43.111 --> 00:09:44.840
What was your address?

167
00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.000
Where was your house?

168
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:47.716
<v BRAXTON>Oh, Lord.

169
00:09:47.716 --> 00:09:51.657
Now see that’s a problem because I can’t remember the address.

170
00:09:51.657 --> 00:09:56.672
<v CRAMER>Well give us a general . . . Like maybe like a point of reference or something.

171
00:09:56.672 --> 00:09:57.030
<v BRAXTON>It’s . . .

172
00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:58.821
<v CRAMER>Is it close to here?

173
00:09:58.821 --> 00:10:02.045
Is it close to where we are right now?

174
00:10:02.045 --> 00:10:04.910
<v BRAXTON>I’d say about a mile down the road.

175
00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:05.269
<v CRAMER>Okay.

176
00:10:05.269 --> 00:10:05.985
Which way?

177
00:10:05.985 --> 00:10:09.209
Going if we go out of here and go . . .

178
00:10:09.209 --> 00:10:09.925
<v BRAXTON>Going west.

179
00:10:09.925 --> 00:10:11.000
Go west.

180
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.000
<v CRAMER>Is that left or right?

181
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:13.400
<v BRAXTON>West.

182
00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:14.200
<v CRAMER>I don’t . . .

183
00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:15.000
<v BRAXTON>West.

184
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:15.635
<v CRAMER>Okay.

185
00:10:15.635 --> 00:10:28.333
<v BRAXTON>I was in that area where a lot of my other cousins were staying [Punkie?] Prater, and Pat Prater, and [?],

186
00:10:28.333 --> 00:10:43.571
and D.D. and Uncle Paul, Aunt [P?] and we all stayed about the same area but it was right next door from each other.

187
00:10:43.571 --> 00:11:01.984
And they had kids but I don’t know my mom and them just isn’t [. . .?] and I guess if probably [?] but still they wouldn’t let me play with other kids.

188
00:11:01.984 --> 00:11:13.413
Because I guess the other kids was bad influence, but this used to be a healthy little town.

189
00:11:13.413 --> 00:11:20.397
It was a vicious little town because we used to fight.

190
00:11:20.397 --> 00:11:24.206
I mean we used to fight.

191
00:11:24.206 --> 00:11:28.651
Even as young kids we would fight.

192
00:11:28.651 --> 00:11:35.000
Give me something else to go on because I’m . . .

193
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:41.289
<v CRAMER>So you said you went to church in Mount . . . at Mount Zion.

194
00:11:41.289 --> 00:11:46.005
Who were some of the leaders of that church?

195
00:11:46.005 --> 00:11:47.053
<v BRAXTON>Oh, man.

196
00:11:47.053 --> 00:11:48.102
Reverend Washington.

197
00:11:48.102 --> 00:11:50.198
He was the minister.

198
00:11:50.198 --> 00:11:51.246
Oh, man.

199
00:11:51.246 --> 00:12:06.968
There’s so many . . . Auntie [?] used to sing in the choir and so many of my people used to . . . Well I come from a family that does a lot of singing.

200
00:12:06.968 --> 00:12:09.064
Even like Tony Braxton.

201
00:12:09.064 --> 00:12:13.781
My dad and them, they had a little group.

202
00:12:13.781 --> 00:12:18.497
It was a quartet, but it was very good.

203
00:12:18.497 --> 00:12:20.070
It was good.

204
00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:26.882
All my cousins was in it . . . I say about six maybe seven guys.

205
00:12:26.882 --> 00:12:28.979
And it was good.

206
00:12:28.979 --> 00:12:32.647
And my dad really could sing, man.

207
00:12:32.647 --> 00:12:43.652
When he sing he would have the church . . . Everybody in the congregation would stand up and be just enjoying his self.

208
00:12:43.652 --> 00:12:46.273
Because he used to blow.

209
00:12:46.273 --> 00:12:57.278
Because he was shorter than any . . . because even my grandmother and them all was shorter than . . . Like some little [piglins?], man.

210
00:12:57.278 --> 00:13:06.711
The tallest person that was in my family was my Uncle June, which his name was Nolan Braxton.

211
00:13:06.711 --> 00:13:08.807
Named after my grandfather.

212
00:13:08.807 --> 00:13:09.332
But . . .

213
00:13:09.332 --> 00:13:10.904
<v CRAMER>Did you sing?

214
00:13:10.904 --> 00:13:13.000
Do you sing?

215
00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:13.984
<v BRAXTON>I sings.

216
00:13:13.984 --> 00:13:19.885
At the church I belong to which is Willow Springs Baptist Church.

217
00:13:19.885 --> 00:13:26.279
That’s way out on Willow Springs . . . Well it’s off of High Hope Road.

218
00:13:26.279 --> 00:13:31.689
And I’ve been staying there for . . . Oh, a good time now.

219
00:13:31.689 --> 00:13:35.623
Ever since I come back from Houston [Texas].

220
00:13:35.623 --> 00:13:37.098
I got married.

221
00:13:37.098 --> 00:13:41.525
Because I think I’m jumping ahead of myself, but . . .

222
00:13:41.525 --> 00:13:43.000
<v CRAMER>That’s okay.

223
00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.232
<v BRAXTON>Let’s go on with some of your questions.

224
00:13:46.232 --> 00:13:47.040
<v CRAMER>That’s okay.

225
00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:50.273
Okay so where did you go to school?

226
00:13:50.273 --> 00:13:53.505
<v BRAXTON>I went to school right here at Mossville.

227
00:13:53.505 --> 00:13:55.525
I played in the band.

228
00:13:55.525 --> 00:13:58.758
I had a B to a C average.

229
00:13:58.758 --> 00:14:03.202
I never was an A student because . . . I mean I’m smart.

230
00:14:03.202 --> 00:14:11.687
I was smart and everything, but I fell in with some guys and those guys had me smoking and . . . Smoking cigarettes.

231
00:14:11.687 --> 00:14:16.131
I was maybe twelve years old when I started smoking cigarettes.

232
00:14:16.131 --> 00:14:18.152
I was in the band.

233
00:14:18.152 --> 00:14:20.576
I was football . . . I played football.

234
00:14:20.576 --> 00:14:23.000
<v CRAMER>What instrument did you play?

235
00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.807
<v BRAXTON>I was trumpet . . . Played trumpet.

236
00:14:25.807 --> 00:14:26.368
<v CRAMER>Okay.

237
00:14:26.368 --> 00:14:36.474
<v BRAXTON>And in gym I was real good in my gym class because . . . I’d say about the sixth grade.

238
00:14:36.474 --> 00:14:39.281
Sixth to seventh, eighth, ninth,

239
00:14:39.281 --> 00:14:49.947
tenth . . . About close to the eleventh grade I was the fastest thing in the school in running track and . . .

240
00:14:49.947 --> 00:14:55.000
<v CRAMER>So what positon did you play in football?

241
00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:56.733
<v BRAXTON>I was the half-back.

242
00:14:56.733 --> 00:14:57.600
<v CRAMER>Oh, okay.

243
00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:00.633
Is that . . . That’s on the offense, right?

244
00:15:00.633 --> 00:15:03.233
Or is that on the defense?

245
00:15:03.233 --> 00:15:03.667
<v BRAXTON>[Agrees].

246
00:15:03.667 --> 00:15:04.100
<v CRAMER>Okay.

247
00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:08.000
<v BRAXTON>I played with . . . My coach was Coach Williams.

248
00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.714
<v CRAMER>The one that they’re having the . . .

249
00:15:09.714 --> 00:15:10.000
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

250
00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.143
<v CRAMER>Celebration for right now?

251
00:15:11.143 --> 00:15:11.429
<v BRAXTON>Right.

252
00:15:11.429 --> 00:15:12.000
Right.

253
00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:12.398
<v CRAMER>Okay.

254
00:15:12.398 --> 00:15:15.585
What can you tell us about Coach Williams?

255
00:15:15.585 --> 00:15:17.577
<v BRAXTON>Oh Coach Williams was great.

256
00:15:17.577 --> 00:15:19.569
He was a strict guy.

257
00:15:19.569 --> 00:15:29.927
Because now, if a fight would break out in school, Coach would grab you by the ear, both guys or whoever they is, women, or girls.

258
00:15:29.927 --> 00:15:37.098
And he’d put the boxing gloves on them and we’d go behind the gym and we would box.

259
00:15:37.098 --> 00:15:39.488
You’ll box until you get tired.

260
00:15:39.488 --> 00:15:46.659
Until you tired enough where you can hug the other person and you could . . . It’s all over then.

261
00:15:46.659 --> 00:15:49.049
But Coach was a good guy.

262
00:15:49.049 --> 00:15:50.642
Still a good guy.

263
00:15:50.642 --> 00:15:51.837
<v CRAMER>Sounds like it.

264
00:15:51.837 --> 00:15:57.415
I mean y’all are getting ready to have a big . . . You’re honoring him tomorrow.

265
00:15:57.415 --> 00:15:58.211
It’s tomorrow . . .?

266
00:15:58.211 --> 00:15:58.610
<v BRAXTON>[Agrees].

267
00:15:58.610 --> 00:15:59.407
<v CRAMER>Or Sunday?

268
00:15:59.407 --> 00:16:01.000
It’s on Sunday?

269
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:02.858
<v BRAXTON>I think it’s tomorrow.

270
00:16:02.858 --> 00:16:05.180
Yes it’s one of them.

271
00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:05.645
<v CRAMER>Okay.

272
00:16:05.645 --> 00:16:15.398
So what places were some of the most [yelling in background] hold on just a second they’re being kind of loud.

273
00:16:15.398 --> 00:16:21.900
What places were the most important places in Mossville when you were growing up?

274
00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:23.758
<v BRAXTON>Well, for one, home.

275
00:16:23.758 --> 00:16:24.223
<v CRAMER>Okay.

276
00:16:24.223 --> 00:16:27.938
<v BRAXTON>Home was great because I was very secure.

277
00:16:27.938 --> 00:16:33.512
I didn’t have no drive by shootings like what goes on now.

278
00:16:33.512 --> 00:16:36.763
And people wasn’t jumping through your window.

279
00:16:36.763 --> 00:16:44.194
You could leave your window down, or your door open and go to sleep at night.

280
00:16:44.194 --> 00:16:45.588
Everything was good.

281
00:16:45.588 --> 00:16:55.341
Only bad part about it is you have outsiders that come in, and to me back then Mossville didn’t like outsiders.

282
00:16:55.341 --> 00:17:01.379
Even though we go to school here . . . and take for instance Lake Charles.

283
00:17:01.379 --> 00:17:11.133
We would play Lake Charles, or we would play Sulphur in a game, but we couldn’t go there and talk to

284
00:17:11.133 --> 00:17:12.062
<c.p>their girls.

285
00:17:12.062 --> 00:17:13.455
They would fight.

286
00:17:13.455 --> 00:17:14.848
We would fight.

287
00:17:14.848 --> 00:17:24.137
And they sure couldn’t come here and talk to the girls here, and we had some fine girls here, man.

288
00:17:24.137 --> 00:17:25.995
They couldn’t come here.

289
00:17:25.995 --> 00:17:28.782
But it . . . It was all good.

290
00:17:28.782 --> 00:17:29.711
Real good.

291
00:17:29.711 --> 00:17:31.104
I liked it.

292
00:17:31.104 --> 00:17:35.749
<v CRAMER>So where did you . . . Where did the young folks gather?

293
00:17:35.749 --> 00:17:39.000
I mean what was the places . . .

294
00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:42.929
<v BRAXTON>We had a little hall right down there by the track.

295
00:17:42.929 --> 00:17:50.429
I guess we called it the City Hall, but it was a little hall that used to give dances and whatever.

296
00:17:50.429 --> 00:17:55.786
And then we had the Paradise Inn which was located right there on Prater Road.

297
00:17:55.786 --> 00:17:56.857
<v CRAMER>What was that?

298
00:17:56.857 --> 00:17:57.571
<v BRAXTON>Paradise Inn.

299
00:17:57.571 --> 00:17:59.000
<v CRAMER>Oh, Paradise Inn.

300
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.433
<v BRAXTON>And because Valery Montgomery owned that.

301
00:18:02.433 --> 00:18:09.299
We had Dave Ross, he had a . . . Dave Ross is my father-in-law.

302
00:18:09.299 --> 00:18:14.448
He’s deceased now, but I married to his daughter.

303
00:18:14.448 --> 00:18:15.593
Patsy Ross.

304
00:18:15.593 --> 00:18:25.892
And he had a little canteen with . . . he had a jukebox in it, and a little pinball machine.

305
00:18:25.892 --> 00:18:28.180
And you drank sodas.

306
00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:39.624
Every now and then you’d sneak in a . . . well back then we wasn’t drinking . . . Jack Daniel’s or nothing like that.

307
00:18:39.624 --> 00:18:41.912
We was drinking wine.

308
00:18:41.912 --> 00:18:45.345
Like Boon Farm and Valley High.

309
00:18:45.345 --> 00:18:50.495
Yeah and we’d mix that stuff with Kool-Aid, man.

310
00:18:50.495 --> 00:18:52.784
They . . . It was on.

311
00:18:52.784 --> 00:18:53.356
It

312
00:18:53.356 --> 00:18:55.072
<c.p>was really on.

313
00:18:55.072 --> 00:19:01.366
And . . . but we would have fights over there at Dave Ross’.

314
00:19:01.366 --> 00:19:07.088
And we had another place which was the Cowboy Inn.

315
00:19:07.088 --> 00:19:11.665
Which my aunt, my dad’s sister owned that.

316
00:19:11.665 --> 00:19:14.526
Which her name is [Luticia?].

317
00:19:14.526 --> 00:19:16.242
Was [Luticia?] Rigmaiden.

318
00:19:16.242 --> 00:19:25.969
And of course it was located behind the school behind Mossville School, back up in that area.

319
00:19:25.969 --> 00:19:28.258
She had [. . .?] as well.

320
00:19:28.258 --> 00:19:33.407
She had gave it up a long time ago,

321
00:19:33.407 --> 00:19:43.134
but she’s passed away too. [Behind?] a lot of this stuff which I . . . and I miss her.

322
00:19:43.134 --> 00:19:50.000
But give me something else, baby, because it’s a sad feeling.

323
00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:53.574
<v CRAMER>Well I could listen to you talk.

324
00:19:53.574 --> 00:19:56.637
You have a . . . Lovely, lovely mannerisms.

325
00:19:56.637 --> 00:20:06.849
I have some other questions for you and we’ll wrap it up, but . . . so what does home mean to you?

326
00:20:06.849 --> 00:20:08.891
<v BRAXTON>Home mean being secure.

327
00:20:08.891 --> 00:20:11.954
Means that I can go outside.

328
00:20:11.954 --> 00:20:15.528
I get me a little drink on.

329
00:20:15.528 --> 00:20:24.718
Go outside turn my music up and sing as loud as I want because I’m away from everybody.

330
00:20:24.718 --> 00:20:28.803
I don’t like to be close . . . so close.

331
00:20:28.803 --> 00:20:41.056
My wife gets on me all the time because I’ll be out there playing with the lawnmowers or something . . . Just start digging the shrubbery.

332
00:20:41.056 --> 00:20:46.673
And I’m just singing and drinking and having a nice time.

333
00:20:46.673 --> 00:20:49.736
And home makes me feel . . . Family.

334
00:20:49.736 --> 00:20:51.268
Your family’s there.

335
00:20:51.268 --> 00:20:55.863
I got one daughter that was . . . stays with me.

336
00:20:55.863 --> 00:20:58.415
Is staying with me now.

337
00:20:58.415 --> 00:21:01.989
Her name is Tamika and she’s thirty-one.

338
00:21:01.989 --> 00:21:08.627
My other two sons, one is in Tennessee, the other is in Seattle.

339
00:21:08.627 --> 00:21:12.201
The one in Tennessee is an engineer.

340
00:21:12.201 --> 00:21:13.732
He’s driving trains.

341
00:21:13.732 --> 00:21:19.859
The other one that’s in Seattle [Washington], I put it like this.

342
00:21:19.859 --> 00:21:22.923
He’s kind of like Fred Sanford.

343
00:21:22.923 --> 00:21:25.475
But he’s makes this money.

344
00:21:25.475 --> 00:21:33.134
He’s . . . I would say he’s a coordinator because he pick up junk iron and whatever.

345
00:21:33.134 --> 00:21:43.345
But he makes something . . . No more than about I think five, six thousand dollars a week picking up junk iron.

346
00:21:43.345 --> 00:21:47.940
Well prices are higher there than they are here.

347
00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:48.451
So.

348
00:21:48.451 --> 00:21:50.493
Give me something else.

349
00:21:50.493 --> 00:22:02.746
<v CRAMER>What’s the most important thing that you want people to know about Mossville that’s not in the history books and not written down anywhere?

350
00:22:02.746 --> 00:22:06.831
<v BRAXTON>You missed it if you didn’t stay here.

351
00:22:06.831 --> 00:22:09.384
That’s the most important thing.

352
00:22:09.384 --> 00:22:11.937
You missed a lovely place.

353
00:22:11.937 --> 00:22:15.000
It was a great place.

354
00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:18.358
<v CRAMER>What about it makes it great?

355
00:22:18.358 --> 00:22:19.477
<v BRAXTON>The people.

356
00:22:19.477 --> 00:22:22.275
The people all stuck together.

357
00:22:22.275 --> 00:22:25.633
If . . . See we had an incident.

358
00:22:25.633 --> 00:22:28.991
Show you what I’m talking about.

359
00:22:28.991 --> 00:22:31.229
We had an incident.

360
00:22:31.229 --> 00:22:36.266
There was a store that was on Prater Street.

361
00:22:36.266 --> 00:22:38.505
We called it Rougeau.

362
00:22:38.505 --> 00:22:43.541
Okay, and they was . . . It was run by white.

363
00:22:43.541 --> 00:22:45.780
Okay, they moved in.

364
00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:49.138
They were staying in the store.

365
00:22:49.138 --> 00:22:51.376
The store’s in front.

366
00:22:51.376 --> 00:22:58.651
Then their home was in the back, I guess was their living quarters.

367
00:22:58.651 --> 00:23:08.165
And one day a little black girl walks in and the store owner slapped the little girl.

368
00:23:08.165 --> 00:23:10.963
So the word got out.

369
00:23:10.963 --> 00:23:12.083
We boycotted.

370
00:23:12.083 --> 00:23:17.679
And just sticking together like that when things go wrong.

371
00:23:17.679 --> 00:23:24.394
I mean it wasn’t a violent thing or anything, but we boycotted.

372
00:23:24.394 --> 00:23:29.431
I mean it was on national news I know.

373
00:23:29.431 --> 00:23:32.789
And I just love the people.

374
00:23:32.789 --> 00:23:40.064
The people that was here was great, but then things started going bad.

375
00:23:40.064 --> 00:23:50.697
People start dying, and the plant start getting the best of us, and things just . . . Everything just moves on.

376
00:23:50.697 --> 00:23:52.936
Which I hated that.

377
00:23:52.936 --> 00:24:02.450
I hate that the people of the plants didn’t try to help more than what they did.

378
00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:09.165
Because we would have had some people still here to tell stories.

379
00:24:09.165 --> 00:24:17.000
Now it’s you guys looking at me and I’m not a good storyteller.

380
00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:20.456
<v CRAMER>Well I beg to differ on that.

381
00:24:20.456 --> 00:24:27.369
And we really appreciate you sitting down with us and talking and telling stories.

382
00:24:27.369 --> 00:24:37.739
And one last question for you, is there anything you want to add as we close this interview part one up?

383
00:24:37.739 --> 00:24:45.639
<v BRAXTON>No more than I hope that this thing do come off as a tribute to Mossville.

384
00:24:45.639 --> 00:24:58.971
As a reminder the people that this was a historical place because we had a guy that I think was the father of Mossville was Pete Moss.

385
00:24:58.971 --> 00:25:05.390
And he used to come down the road with his horse, his wagon.

386
00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:09.834
Of course now he didn’t have the wagon wheels.

387
00:25:09.834 --> 00:25:12.303
He had rubber tire wheels.

388
00:25:12.303 --> 00:25:18.228
And looked like he had about fifteen to thirty dogs behind him.

389
00:25:18.228 --> 00:25:26.622
Running behind him while him and his wife Virginia was riding up on the [buck?] boy seat.

390
00:25:26.622 --> 00:25:30.079
And they’ll go down to the store.

391
00:25:30.079 --> 00:25:34.029
Could really go anywhere they wanted to go.

392
00:25:34.029 --> 00:25:36.004
Go fishing or whatever.

393
00:25:36.004 --> 00:25:42.423
Go back down toward Mount Zion because he stayed across from Mount Zion.

394
00:25:42.423 --> 00:25:45.386
And he wouldn’t mess with nobody.

395
00:25:45.386 --> 00:25:47.855
He wouldn’t talk to anybody.

396
00:25:47.855 --> 00:25:58.224
And the people . . . the kids was always scared of him because he’s always dirty looking, but he was a good man.

397
00:25:58.224 --> 00:26:02.668
To me, I think that he was the founder.

398
00:26:02.668 --> 00:26:09.581
He might been one of the first that was here because he was old.

399
00:26:09.581 --> 00:26:12.050
Now how old was he?

400
00:26:12.050 --> 00:26:13.531
I don’t know.

401
00:26:13.531 --> 00:26:16.000
But I love Mossville.

402
00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:16.460
<v CRAMER>Well . . .

403
00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:24.277
<v BRAXTON>I wouldn’t change nothing that went on back then because it was a learning experience for me.

404
00:26:24.277 --> 00:26:29.796
Because it brought me up was so I’ve never been to jail.

405
00:26:29.796 --> 00:26:32.555
Even though I fought and everything.

406
00:26:32.555 --> 00:26:40.372
Even though we tore up the Cal Cam Fair because policemans . . . We did a lot of stuff.

407
00:26:40.372 --> 00:26:44.051
There’s a lot of stuff that went on.

408
00:26:44.051 --> 00:26:46.810
We hit policemans with billy clubs

409
00:26:46.810 --> 00:26:48.190
<c.p>and oh, man.

410
00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:59.226
Bust the head and we run down the . . . We jumped in the truck we run back to Mossville and hide all under the houses.

411
00:26:59.226 --> 00:27:01.985
Oh we did some stuff, man.

412
00:27:01.985 --> 00:27:03.825
My brother in law,

413
00:27:03.825 --> 00:27:13.022
he went to jail for . . . I think it was a hundred and sixty some days for messing the policeman up.

414
00:27:13.022 --> 00:27:19.000
But he messed him up . . . I mean he messed him up bad.

415
00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:28.771
<v CRAMER>So one of the things we’re going to do is probably come back if you’ll have us back and finish this interview because I’d love to kind of . . . We kind of skipped over a bunch of stuff here.

416
00:27:28.771 --> 00:27:29.029
<v BRAXTON>[Agrees].

417
00:27:29.029 --> 00:27:37.514
<v CRAMER>But I’d love to go back and talk a little bit more about your earliest memories and what Mossville was like so we can help paint that picture and fill in those blanks.

418
00:27:37.514 --> 00:27:40.086
<v BRAXTON>If you outline what you want me to talk about . . .

419
00:27:40.086 --> 00:27:40.600
<v CRAMER>Yes, sir.

420
00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:41.886
<v BRAXTON>We can get it straight.

421
00:27:41.886 --> 00:27:43.171
But if you don’t outline . . .

422
00:27:43.171 --> 00:27:44.457
<v CRAMER>You want to be prepared.

423
00:27:44.457 --> 00:27:46.000
You’d like to be prepared?

424
00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.740
<v BRAXTON>I don’t like to be prepared.

425
00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:52.379
I mean I like . . . if you ask me a question I can get right to it,

426
00:27:52.379 --> 00:27:55.858
but if I got to search my mind and I jump around.

427
00:27:55.858 --> 00:27:58.757
And I don’t want to jump around I want to . . .

428
00:27:58.757 --> 00:28:02.527
<v CRAMER>This is . . . What you’ve done here today is precisely what we’re looking for,

429
00:28:02.527 --> 00:28:09.195
which is why I kind of didn’t interrupt you or didn’t kind of bump in because I . . . What you’re saying is so important.

430
00:28:09.195 --> 00:28:12.385
And the way that you tell that story is absolutely perfect.

431
00:28:12.385 --> 00:28:16.734
In fact, I mean we kind of wish everybody told stories like that, don’t we?

432
00:28:16.734 --> 00:28:23.112
So I mean you made my job easy and I promise next time we’ll make your job a little bit easier, too.

433
00:28:23.112 --> 00:28:28.331
So we will . . . We’ll definitely provide you like a list of topics and stuff that we’re interested in.

434
00:28:28.331 --> 00:28:28.621
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

435
00:28:28.621 --> 00:28:34.130
<v CRAMER>But just to give you an example, some people talked about for example home remedies, gardening by the moon . . .

436
00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:35.000
<v BRAXTON>Oh, man.

437
00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:35.286
<v CRAMER>Right?

438
00:28:35.286 --> 00:28:35.571
Right?

439
00:28:35.571 --> 00:28:39.000
So those are the kinds of things that we want to get.

440
00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:45.571
And today was just kind of a . . . we want to come in and just get to know you and get a little bit,

441
00:28:45.571 --> 00:28:50.143
and then come back and get some of those stories when we have a whole hour,

442
00:28:50.143 --> 00:28:53.857
hour and a half to talk to you about all kinds of stuff.

443
00:28:53.857 --> 00:28:56.143
So . . . Look at all these questions right here.

444
00:28:56.143 --> 00:28:57.000
I had a

445
00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:03.286
<c.p>bunch and I just skipped over them because I really want the opportunity to come back and do more with you, so.

446
00:29:03.286 --> 00:29:05.286
<v BRAXTON>I remember when this road was rock.

447
00:29:05.286 --> 00:29:06.714
It was a rocky road.

448
00:29:06.714 --> 00:29:08.143
It wasn’t paved or nothing.

449
00:29:08.143 --> 00:29:11.000
And we used to race horses down this road.

450
00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:11.419
<v CRAMER>Really?

451
00:29:11.419 --> 00:29:11.839
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

452
00:29:11.839 --> 00:29:16.032
You had a thoroughbred, you put him on the street.

453
00:29:16.032 --> 00:29:19.387
You’re going to see how good he is.

454
00:29:19.387 --> 00:29:21.484
<v CRAMER>When were the roads paved?

455
00:29:21.484 --> 00:29:29.452
Right about maybe the . . . Somebody told me earlier today and I can’t remember myself, but when were they rock?

456
00:29:29.452 --> 00:29:32.806
How long . . . like when are you talking about?

457
00:29:32.806 --> 00:29:34.484
Like what time period . . .

458
00:29:34.484 --> 00:29:35.742
<v BRAXTON>I’m talking about . . .

459
00:29:35.742 --> 00:29:37.000
<v CRAMER>The 60s?

460
00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:37.558
<v BRAXTON>Yes.

461
00:29:37.558 --> 00:29:39.233
Somewhere in there.

462
00:29:39.233 --> 00:29:43.140
Because I’m sixty-three, so right about then.

463
00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:52.070
Because they didn’t have no pave . . . when I was crawfishing in that ditch it wasn’t paved.

464
00:29:52.070 --> 00:29:59.884
No because you got to watch out for the rocks when the cars pass.

465
00:29:59.884 --> 00:30:01.000
<v CRAMER>Oh!

466
00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:07.052
<v BRAXTON>Because their . . . The tires will pop them out, but it was all good.

467
00:30:07.052 --> 00:30:09.379
<v CRAMER>Well thank you so much.

468
00:30:09.379 --> 00:30:10.310
Go ahead . . .

469
00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:13.103
<v BRAXTON>We had some big crawfish, too.

470
00:30:13.103 --> 00:30:14.034
Oh, man.

471
00:30:14.034 --> 00:30:17.293
It was some big red claws, them.

472
00:30:17.293 --> 00:30:29.397
And my mom used to tear my butt because she used to find them under the ice box, or under the table, and they’d be dead.

473
00:30:29.397 --> 00:30:32.655
She’d be wondering what that smell is.

474
00:30:32.655 --> 00:30:35.448
<v CRAMER>It was you terrorizing your sister.

475
00:30:35.448 --> 00:30:42.897
<v BRAXTON>Yeah I done thought in my head must be another dead crawfish somewhere I didn’t get.

476
00:30:42.897 --> 00:30:44.759
But it’s all good.

477
00:30:44.759 --> 00:30:47.086
I loved everything about Mossville.

478
00:30:47.086 --> 00:30:56.862
I wish I can find another town that’s like it, but it didn’t have that much of a chemical problem whatever.

479
00:30:56.862 --> 00:31:03.845
But I don’t think you can find that because the [?] the thing done took over.

480
00:31:03.845 --> 00:31:12.224
Things have to move on I guess, and you got to be with it, or be without it.

481
00:31:12.224 --> 00:31:14.086
You can’t stop progress.

482
00:31:14.086 --> 00:31:16.414
Even though you want to.

483
00:31:16.414 --> 00:31:31.310
And I’m pretty sure somehow or another I may have to move from where I’m staying at because they got to build some more energy . . . I don’t know how you say it,

484
00:31:31.310 --> 00:31:31.776
but . . .

485
00:31:31.776 --> 00:31:32.707
<v CRAMER>Like processing?

486
00:31:32.707 --> 00:31:33.172
<v BRAXTON>Units.

487
00:31:33.172 --> 00:31:39.224
Units for making more energy for the incoming people that’s coming in here.

488
00:31:39.224 --> 00:31:43.414
And I stay way out there by Gulf State.

489
00:31:43.414 --> 00:31:46.672
I don’t know if you know this.

490
00:31:46.672 --> 00:31:49.000
On Houston River Road.

491
00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:49.200
<v CRAMER>A little bit.

492
00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:50.413
<v BRAXTON>[?]

493
00:31:50.413 --> 00:31:50.885
<v CRAMER>[Agrees].

494
00:31:50.885 --> 00:31:54.183
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, well I stay behind that road.

495
00:31:54.183 --> 00:32:00.779
And I don’t know what I’m going to do when it’s come my time.

496
00:32:00.779 --> 00:32:09.260
I know the people that was here, a lot of them was too old to be moving then.

497
00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:21.038
And I know they didn’t get the right money that they supposed to . . . They needed to get to make the rest of their life comfortable.

498
00:32:21.038 --> 00:32:22.923
Yeah, and that’s hurting.

499
00:32:22.923 --> 00:32:27.635
That really hurts because these are some good people here.

500
00:32:27.635 --> 00:32:28.577
I’m sorry.

501
00:32:28.577 --> 00:32:29.519
<v CRAMER>It’s okay.

502
00:32:29.519 --> 00:32:30.933
Speak your mind.

503
00:32:30.933 --> 00:32:38.000
Well I appreciate your enthusiasm in being interviewed and for sharing with us today.

504
00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:39.488
<v BRAXTON>Thank you.

505
00:32:39.488 --> 00:32:45.442
<v CRAMER>Is there something else you want to say?

506
00:32:45.442 --> 00:32:53.628
<v BRAXTON>No. I’m kind of messed up right now because this hurts.

507
00:32:53.628 --> 00:33:10.744
And I know a lot of people that you’ve interviewed or have talked to, they probably feel the same way as I feel.

508
00:33:10.744 --> 00:33:18.930
But it’s a hurting thing you got to forget your home.

509
00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.907
Where you come from.

510
00:33:21.907 --> 00:33:24.884
All the good times.

511
00:33:24.884 --> 00:33:27.116
The bad times.

512
00:33:27.116 --> 00:33:28.605
They matters.

513
00:33:28.605 --> 00:33:35.302
They may not seem like it, but they matters.

514
00:33:35.302 --> 00:33:39.023
I’m going to stop now.

515
00:33:39.023 --> 00:33:42.000
Thank you though.

516
00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:42.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4398.

517
00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:42.200
End Session I.]
